# Blur Slider Demo

مشروع Android بسيط بلغة Kotlin لتجربة اختيار صورة والتحكم في قوة Blur بواسطة SeekBar.

## ما الذي يوضحه المشروع؟

يستخدم المشروع `ImageView` لعرض الصورة المختارة، و`SeekBar` لتغيير نصف قطر التمويه لحظيًا. عند اختيار قيمة صفر تعود الصورة إلى وضعها الطبيعي.

المشروع لا يستخدم Chaquopy أو Python أو ML Kit؛ وهو مقصود كتجربة صغيرة لفهم فصل واجهة المستخدم عن مرحلة معالجة الصور قبل إضافة العزل وخريطة العمق.

## المتطلبات

- Android Studio حديث أو بيئة Gradle حقيقية.
- Android SDK 34.
- جهاز أو محاكي Android 12 أو أحدث، لأن `RenderEffect` يحتاج API 31 أو أعلى.

## التشغيل

افتح مجلد المشروع في Android Studio، ثم نفّذ Gradle Sync وشغّل نسخة Debug على جهاز Android. اضغط «اختيار صورة»، اختر صورة من المعرض، ثم حرّك شريط «قوة البلور».

## الملفات المهمة

- `app/src/main/java/com/example/blurslider/MainActivity.kt`: اختيار الصورة وتطبيق RenderEffect.
- `app/src/main/res/layout/activity_main.xml`: الواجهة.
- `app/build.gradle`: إعداد Android وKotlin.

## ملاحظة

هذا Blur يطبّق على الصورة كلها. الخطوة التالية في مشروع العزل هي استبدال التأثير العام بقناع للشخص أو خريطة عمق، ثم تطبيق التمويه على الخلفية فقط.

## MiDaS depth preview

The project now includes `midas_small_256_fp16.tflite` in `app/src/main/assets` and a `MidasDepthRunner` using the LiteRT CompiledModel Kotlin API. Choose an image, then press `خريطة العمق`. The preview is a normalized grayscale inverse-depth map: brighter regions are generally nearer and darker regions farther away.

This checkpoint intentionally runs MiDaS on the CPU first for compatibility. After verifying that the output is correct on the target device, the accelerator can be changed to GPU and benchmarked. The depth preview is separate from the global Blur slider; depth-aware background blur is the next stage and requires a mask-aware GPU pipeline.

The model expects RGB NHWC float32 input with ImageNet normalization and produces a 256x256 relative inverse-depth output, as documented by the model publisher.

## Portrait Blur stage

The Portrait Blur button runs MiDaS once, normalizes its 256x256 inverse-depth map, and creates a lightweight preview. The preview keeps generally nearer pixels sharper and blends farther pixels with a fast downscale/upscale blur. The slider changes the background blur strength without rerunning MiDaS, although this educational CPU implementation is not yet the final 60fps GPU shader pipeline.

The current depth segmentation is approximate because MiDaS supplies relative depth rather than a person segmentation mask. Hair, hands, objects at similar depth, and complex backgrounds may require a later ML Kit mask and edge refinement stage.

The Choreographer warning about a frame timestamp being in the future is a device graphics/vsync warning. If the app remains responsive and no visible rendering problem occurs, it can be ignored during this prototype stage.

## Portrait blur fix

The portrait processor now estimates a scene-relative focus depth instead of using a fixed brightness threshold. It also keeps a small baseline blur weight and increases the preview blur range, so moving the slider to the maximum produces a clearly visible effect even when MiDaS finds weak depth separation. The slider still does not rerun MiDaS; it only re-renders from the cached depth map.

For the clearest test, select a photo with a person close to the camera and a background several meters away, press `عزل Portrait`, then move the slider all the way to the right. This is still a CPU preview and an approximate depth effect, not the final ML Kit alpha mask plus GPU shader implementation.

## Performance and Exynos note

The depth model is explicitly requested on the CPU in this prototype. LiteRT may still print an optional NPU auto-registration warning during initialization; on devices where the accelerator provider is not available, this is non-fatal and CPU execution continues.

The portrait preview now prepares the reduced image and its blurred copy once after MiDaS finishes. Slider changes only blend cached bitmaps against the cached depth map, instead of rebuilding the blur for every movement. This reduces stutter considerably, although a production 60fps implementation should move the compositing step to OpenGL ES/GLSL.

## Person mask stage

This version adds ML Kit Selfie Segmentation in addition to MiDaS. ML Kit produces a foreground-confidence mask, while MiDaS provides relative depth. The final preview protects pixels with high person confidence and uses depth to vary the background blur. ML Kit's bundled selfie segmenter is intended for on-device segmentation and supports raw-size masks; the project uses single-image mode for gallery photos.
